query:
Synthetic mouse input dispatches mouseout to a node re-appended while hovered; real Chrome does not

### Version

1.62.1

### Steps to reproduce

When an element re-appends itself to its parent inside its own `mouseover` handler (the d3 "move to front" idiom), real Chrome never dispatches `mouseout` to that element afterwards — but through Playwright's input path it does. The result is that a real, user-visible bug cannot be reproduced in a Playwright test: the test observes correct behavior that no real user gets.

**`index.html`** — one box that re-appends itself on `mouseover`, one control that doesn't:

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<style>.box { width: 180px; height: 120px; color: #fff }
  #reappend { background: #b23 } #control { background: #2a7 }
  .row { display: flex; gap: 24px }</style>

<div class="row">
  <div><div class="box" id="reappend">#reappend</div><pre id="out-reappend">over=0 out=0</pre></div>
  <div><div class="box" id="control">#control</div><pre id="out-control">over=0 out=0</pre></div>
</div>

<script>
function instrument(id, reappendOnHover) {
  const el = document.getElementById(id), out = document.getElementById('out-' + id);
  const counts = { over: 0, out: 0 };
  window['counts_' + id] = counts;
  const render = () => out.textContent = `over=${counts.over} out=${counts.out}`;
  el.addEventListener('mouseover', () => {
    counts.over++;
    if (reappendOnHover) el.parentNode.appendChild(el);  // remove + re-insert while hovered
    render();
  });
  el.addEventListener('mouseout', () => { counts.out++; render(); });
}
instrument('reappend', true);
instrument('control', false);
</script>
```

**`repro.cjs`**:

```js
const { chromium } = require('playwright-core');
const path = require('path');

(async () => {
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  console.log('chromium:', browser.version());
  const page = await browser.newPage({ viewport: { width: 800, height: 600 } });
  await page.goto('file://' + path.join(__dirname, 'index.html'));

  for (const id of ['reappend', 'control']) {
    await page.locator('#' + id).hover();
    await page.waitForTimeout(200);
    const hovered = await page.evaluate(i => ({ ...window['counts_' + i] }), id);
    await page.mouse.move(700, 560);            // move clear of both boxes
    await page.waitForTimeout(300);
    const left = await page.evaluate(i => ({ ...window['counts_' + i] }), id);
    console.log(`#${id} after hover: over=${hovered.over} out=${hovered.out}` +
                `  after leaving: over=${left.over} out=${left.out}`);
  }
  await browser.close();
})();
```

To compare against a real browser, open the same `index.html` in Chrome ≥ 144, hover each box, and move the pointer well clear of it.

### Expected behavior

The boundary-event sequence Playwright produces should be one a real pointer can produce. In Chrome ≥ 144 (["boundary event dispatch tracks node removal"](https://developer.chrome.com/release-notes/144), part of Interop 2025), removing a node retargets the pointer to its nearest connected ancestor, so a node that was removed and re-inserted while hovered receives **no** `mouseout`. I'd expect a Playwright-driven pointer to behave the same.

### Actual behavior

Playwright dispatches `mouseout` where a real pointer does not, and additionally treats the re-insertion itself as a leave/re-enter pair.

Real Chrome 148, real pointer:

| element | after hovering | after moving clear |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `#reappend` | `over=1 out=0` | `over=1 out=0` ← **no `mouseout`, ever** |
| `#control` | `over=1 out=0` | `over=1 out=1` |

Playwright 1.62.1 + bundled Chromium 151.0.7922.34, same file:

| element | after hovering | after moving clear |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `#reappend` | `over=2 out=1` ← re-append already produced a leave/enter pair | `over=2 out=2` |
| `#control` | `over=1 out=0` | `over=1 out=1` |

`#control` matches in both, so this is specific to the node that was removed and re-inserted under the pointer, not a general hover discrepancy. Hover *entry* is also identical in both — only the post-mutation bookkeeping differs.

I don't know whether this originates in Playwright or below it in CDP's `Input.dispatchMouseEvent`; if it's the latter, that's still useful to have written down somewhere.

### Additional context

This is not hypothetical. We shipped a d3 visualization whose `mouseover` handler called `parentNode.appendChild(this)` to raise the hovered path. On Chrome ≥ 144 that leaves highlights stuck on, tooltips stranded, and clicks dead — clearly visible to users, and reported by one. Every Playwright test we wrote against those symptoms passed against the known-broken build, on Chromium 145 and 151 alike, at `mouse.move` step counts 1, 5 and 25.

Two details that may help narrow it down:

- **It changed between Playwright releases, with the browser held constant.** Against the same unfixed page and the *same* Chromium 145 binary, the symptom reproduced under `playwright-core` **1.56.1** (highlights stuck, no `mouseout`) and stopped reproducing under **1.62.1**. So something in Playwright's input path changed between those versions. (Under 1.62.1 I checked Chromium 145 and 151 — no difference. Under 1.56.1 I only had 145 to hand, so I can't speak to 1.56.1 + 151.)
- Under 1.62.1 the bundled full Chromium and the headless shell behave identically. I did not test headed mode.

Our workaround was to stop asserting the symptom and assert the DOM mutation instead (a `MutationObserver` proving the hovered node is never detached) — that does discriminate. But it only guards the one line of code, not the behavior, so anything reintroducing this class of bug by another route stays invisible to the suite.

### Environment

```
Playwright: 1.62.1
Chromium:   151.0.7922.34 (bundled) — also checked 145.0.7632.6
Real browser compared against: Chrome 148.0.7778.280
OS:         macOS 26.5.2 (arm64)
Node:       v26.1.0
```

