Sorry but I just realized this looks like a duplicate of https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/issues/28657

- #28657 — same feature request (`Response.dispose` like `APIResponse.dispose`), closed for inactivity. You asked to open a new issue and reference it.
- #6319 — `response.body()` cached until context close; closed after overflow GC (#27315). That GC is still 10k handles/type and does not drop `_contentPromise` for a live context with large bodies.
- microsoft/playwright-python#286 — Request/Response stuck in `Connection._objects` until context close.

The 10k dispatcher cap does not replace a dispose API when each `body()` is several MB.

This is for network-heavy use cases, I really need to be aggressive with GC, and closing/reopening context is not ok for my use case. Some webapps have authentication state that only lives in Javascript context, no localstorage/cookie/sessionstorage. So restoring a storage_state won't work :(

I made a quick hack locally to see how the leak disappears when we remove `_contentPromise`
It's a local webpage, serving a ton of API responses. 40 × 4 MiB JSON, requestfinished → response.body() → json.loads → drop, context stays open.

Current code https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/main/packages/playwright-core/src/server/network.ts#L647

```ts
body() {
  if (!this._contentPromise) {
    this._contentPromise = this._finishedPromise.then(async () => {
      // Network.getResponseBody / _responseBodyOverride
    });
  }
  return this._contentPromise;
}
```

The hack is to never cache. My feature request is to be able to dispose from client-side all the way here.
```ts
body() {
  return this._finishedPromise.then(async () => {
    return this._getResponseBodyCallback(); // same CDP fetch, no assignment
  });
}
```

I'll spare you the hack script but it's basically a python http server to host the page, and then a hacky `.cjs` injected. `NODE_OPTIONS='--expose-gc --require=./drop_body_cache.cjs'`

