[Bug]: With javaScriptEnabled: false, an action retry backoff never completes (in-page setTimeout never fires)

### Version

1.59.1

### Steps to reproduce

With `javaScriptEnabled: false`, an actionability retry that has to wait never finishes waiting. The retry backoff is an in-page `setTimeout`, and timers don't run while script execution is disabled.

Self-contained reproduction (no server; the page is served by `route`):

```js
// repro.mjs — node repro.mjs
import { chromium } from "playwright";

// The button is covered for 300 ms by an overlay that a pure-CSS animation hides; no script involved.
const html = `<!doctype html><style>
#cover{position:fixed;inset:0;background:rgba(0,0,0,.1);animation:gone 0s linear .3s forwards}
@keyframes gone{to{visibility:hidden}}
</style><form action="/done"><button>Continue</button></form><div id="cover"></div>`;

for (const [label, opts] of [["bundled", {}], ["chrome", { channel: "chrome" }]]) {
  const browser = await chromium.launch(opts);
  for (const javaScriptEnabled of [true, false]) {
    const context = await browser.newContext({ javaScriptEnabled });
    await context.route("http://repro.test/**", (r) => r.fulfill({ contentType: "text/html", body: r.request().url().includes("/done") ? "done" : html }));
    const page = await context.newPage();
    await page.goto("http://repro.test/");
    const t = performance.now();
    const outcome = await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Continue" }).click({ timeout: 90_000 }).then(() => "clicked", (e) => e.name);
    console.log(label, browser.version(), `javaScriptEnabled=${javaScriptEnabled}`, outcome, `${((performance.now() - t) / 1000).toFixed(2)}s`);
    await context.close();
  }
  await browser.close();
}
```

### Expected behavior

The click succeeds shortly after the overlay disappears (~0.3–1 s), as it does with JavaScript enabled.

### Actual behavior

| Browser | `javaScriptEnabled: true` | `javaScriptEnabled: false` |
|---|---|---|
| Bundled Chromium headless shell 147.0.7727.15 | 0.34 s | **30.13 s** |
| Google Chrome 153.0.8010.54, headless | 0.38 s | **never completes** (`TimeoutError` at 90 s) |
| Google Chrome 153.0.8010.54, headed | — | **never completes** (90 s timeout; separate run) |

`DEBUG=pw:api,pw:protocol` for the 147 case shows the sequence:

```
pw:api   element is not stable            (or: <div id="cover"> intercepts pointer events)
pw:api retrying click action
pw:api   waiting 20ms
pw:protocol SEND ► Runtime.callFunctionOn (utilityScript.evaluate …)      ← the backoff
… ~30 s, nothing …
pw:protocol ◀ RECV {"error":{"code":-32000,"message":"Promise was collected"}}
pw:api element was detached from the DOM, retrying
```

The backoff is implemented in the page. `lib/server/dom.js` in 1.59.1 (around line 270):

```js
progress.log(`  waiting ${timeout}ms`);
const result2 = await progress.race(this.evaluateInUtility(([injected, node, timeout2]) => new Promise((f) => setTimeout(f, timeout2)), timeout));
```

With script execution disabled, `setTimeout` callbacks don't run, even in the utility world. The promise stays pending until V8 collects it. In the headless shell that took ~30 s, and Playwright then misreports it as the element being detached (compare #41826, where "Promise was collected" surfaced as a navigation error). In Chrome 153 it was never collected within 90 s, so the action times out.

`requestAnimationFrame` in the utility world does still fire in the same page, which is why the stability check itself works and only the backoff hangs.

### Additional context

**Proposed fix:** do the retry backoff on the driver side, e.g. `await progress.wait(timeout)`, instead of evaluating a `setTimeout` promise in the page. That removes the dependency on the page's timers. As a smaller change, map the `Promise was collected` protocol error to a message that says what happened, not "element was detached".

**Workaround we use:** in no-JS tests, assert the button with `expect(button).toBeVisible()` / `toBeEnabled()` (these poll from the driver and aren't affected), then submit natively with `field.press("Enter")`.

### Environment

```
- OS: macOS 27.0 (arm64)
- Node.js: 22.22.0
- Playwright: 1.59.1 (@playwright/test)
- Browsers: bundled chromium-headless-shell 147.0.7727.15; Google Chrome 153.0.8010.54 (channel "chrome")
```

