query:
[Bug]: test.use({ httpCredentials: [a, b] }) uses only the first entry (the 1.63 array form works in the config and in browser.newContext)

### Version

1.63.0

### Environment

- OS: macOS 27.0.1 (arm64)
- Node.js: 26.10.0
- Browser: Chromium 153.0.8010.12 (bundled)

### Steps to reproduce

In an empty folder: `npm init -y`, `npm i -D @playwright/test@1.63.0`, `npx playwright install chromium`. Then add these two files.

`playwright.config.ts`:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  workers: 1,
  reporter: 'list',
  use: {
    // The same array as in the spec, set in the config.
    httpCredentials: [
      { username: 'alice', password: 'alice-pass', origin: 'http://localhost:5701' },
      { username: 'bob', password: 'bob-pass', origin: 'http://localhost:5702' },
    ],
  },
});
```

`credentials.spec.ts`:

```ts
import http from 'node:http';
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

const A = 'http://localhost:5701';
const B = 'http://localhost:5702';
const credentials = [
  { username: 'alice', password: 'alice-pass', origin: A },
  { username: 'bob', password: 'bob-pass', origin: B },
];

// Two servers behind basic auth, each with its own account.
const servers: http.Server[] = [];
test.beforeAll(async () => {
  for (const [port, user, pass] of [[5701, 'alice', 'alice-pass'], [5702, 'bob', 'bob-pass']] as const) {
    const expected = 'Basic ' + Buffer.from(`${user}:${pass}`).toString('base64');
    const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
      if (req.headers.authorization !== expected) {
        res.writeHead(401, { 'WWW-Authenticate': 'Basic realm="test"' });
        return res.end('401');
      }
      res.end(`hello ${user}`);
    });
    await new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.listen(port, resolve));
    servers.push(server);
  }
});
test.afterAll(() => servers.forEach((s) => s.close()));

test('array in the config use: both origins sign in', async ({ page }) => {
  expect((await page.goto(A))!.status()).toBe(200);
  expect((await page.goto(B))!.status()).toBe(200);
});

test('array in browser.newContext: both origins sign in', async ({ browser }) => {
  const context = await browser.newContext({ httpCredentials: credentials });
  const page = await context.newPage();
  expect((await page.goto(A))!.status()).toBe(200);
  expect((await page.goto(B))!.status()).toBe(200);
  await context.close();
});

test.describe('array in test.use', () => {
  test.use({ httpCredentials: credentials });

  test('both origins sign in', async ({ page, httpCredentials }) => {
    console.log('httpCredentials fixture value:', JSON.stringify(httpCredentials));
    expect((await page.goto(A))!.status()).toBe(200);
    expect((await page.goto(B))!.status()).toBe(200);
  });
});
```

Run `npx playwright test`.

### Expected behavior

All three tests pass: the same array means the same thing in the config's `use`, in `test.use()` and in `browser.newContext()`.

[testOptions.httpCredentials](https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-testoptions#test-options-http-credentials):
"Pass an array to use different credentials for different origins. The first entry that matches the request origin is
used, and entries with no origin match any request."

[Release notes 1.63](https://playwright.dev/docs/release-notes#browser-and-context): "httpCredentials now also accepts
an array of credentials."

### Actual behavior

In `test.use()` the fixture receives only the first object of the array, so the second origin gets no credentials
(401). The same array works in the config's `use` and in `browser.newContext()`. TypeScript accepts the `test.use()`
form without an error.

