query:
[Bug]: WebKit: click() on an element two or more shadow roots deep fails with "<outer-host> intercepts pointer events"

### Version

1.63.0 (also `main` at 66096ef78, WebKit r2370)

### Steps to reproduce

1. Save the test below as `repro.spec.ts` (also in this folder).
2. `npx playwright test repro.spec.ts --browser=webkit`

```ts
import { expect, test } from '@playwright/test';

const nested = `
  <outer-host></outer-host>
  <script>
    customElements.define('inner-host', class extends HTMLElement {
      connectedCallback() { this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }).innerHTML = '<input>'; }
    });
    customElements.define('outer-host', class extends HTMLElement {
      connectedCallback() { this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }).innerHTML = '<inner-host></inner-host>'; }
    });
  </script>`;

test('click an input two shadow roots deep', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.setContent(nested);
  const input = page.locator('input');
  await input.click({ timeout: 5000 });
  await expect(input).toBeFocused();
});

test('the click itself reaches the input', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.setContent(nested);
  const input = page.locator('input');
  await input.click({ force: true });
  await expect(input).toBeFocused();
});

test('WebKit puts the outermost host first in elementsFromPoint', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.setContent(nested);
  const tags = await page.locator('input').evaluate((input) => {
    const { x, y, width, height } = input.getBoundingClientRect();
    const root = input.getRootNode() as ShadowRoot;
    return root.elementsFromPoint(x + width / 2, y + height / 2).slice(0, 2).map((e) => e.tagName);
  });
  expect(tags[0]).toBe('INPUT');
});
```

### Expected behavior

`click()` on the `<input>` passes in WebKit, as in Chromium. The element is visible, nothing
covers it, and a click with `force: true` reaches it and focuses it.

### Actual behavior

In WebKit, the first test times out, retrying with:

```
- element is visible, enabled and stable
- scrolling into view if needed
- done scrolling
- <outer-host></outer-host> intercepts pointer events
```

The second test (`force: true`) passes. The third shows the cause: on any shadow root below the
first one, WebKit's `elementsFromPoint()` returns the **outermost** shadow host first, then the
expected chain. `elementFromPoint()` on each root is correct.

| Root | Chromium `elementsFromPoint` | WebKit `elementsFromPoint` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `document` | `OUTER-HOST, BODY, HTML` | `OUTER-HOST, BODY, HTML` |
| `outer-host` shadow root | `INNER-HOST, BODY, HTML` | `OUTER-HOST, INNER-HOST, BODY, HTML` |
| `inner-host` shadow root | `INPUT, INNER-HOST, BODY, HTML` | `OUTER-HOST, INPUT, INNER-HOST, BODY` |

With three levels, every inner root starts with the outermost host as well.

### Additional context

`InjectedScript.expectHitTarget()` (`packages/injected/src/injectedScript.ts`) already works around
this WebKit behaviour since #21642 (fix for #21596), but only when the extra first element is the
host of the root being tested:

```ts
if (elements[0] && elements[0].shadowRoot === root && elements[1] === singleElement) {
  elements.shift();
}
```

That holds for one shadow root. From two roots on, `elements[0]` is the outermost host, whose
`shadowRoot` is a different root, so the check does not fire and the hit target resolves to
`<outer-host>`. Web components that nest other web components (e.g. a design-system input that
keeps its native input in its shadow root, used inside another web component) hit this on every
click in WebKit.

Suggested fix (PR ready): drop the `<host>` when it is the host of the tested root **or of any root
above it**. The roots are already collected in `roots`, so this also works for closed shadow roots:

```ts
if (elements[0] && elements[1] === singleElement && roots.slice(index, -1).some(r => (r as ShadowRoot).host === elements[0]))
  elements.shift();
```

Verified with two new tests in `tests/library/hit-target.spec.ts` (nested open and nested closed
shadow roots): they fail in WebKit without the change and pass with it; the whole
`hit-target.spec.ts` and the page click, check, fill and dispatchEvent specs pass in WebKit and
Chromium.

Workaround until then: `page.mouse.click()` at the center of the element's bounding box.

The root cause is in WebKit (`elementsFromPoint` on a `ShadowRoot`); I have not reported it to
bugs.webkit.org.

### Environment

```
System:
  OS: macOS 27.0
  CPU: (18) arm64 Apple M5 Pro
Binaries:
  Node: 24.15.0
  pnpm: 12.5.1
Languages:
  TypeScript
npmPackages:
  @playwright/test: 1.63.0
```

