[Bug]: after clock.install() + pauseAt(), an init script that reads the time makes the next page run on real time

### Version

1.64.0 (same with 1.63.0)

### Steps to reproduce

Single script, no project needed:

```js
const { chromium } = require('playwright');

(async () => {
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.route('https://example.test/**', route => route.fulfill({ contentType: 'text/html', body: '<p>page</p>' }));

  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2024-02-02T08:00:00Z') });
  await page.clock.pauseAt(new Date('2024-02-02T10:00:00Z'));
  await page.addInitScript(() => { Date.now(); }); // any clock read at document start

  await page.goto('https://example.test/a');
  await page.clock.runFor(1000);                    // any clock call made after the init script was added
  await page.goto('https://example.test/b');

  const before = await page.evaluate(() => Date.now());
  await page.waitForTimeout(1000);
  const after = await page.evaluate(() => Date.now());
  console.log('Date.now() moved by', after - before, 'ms during one real second');
  await browser.close();
})();
```

1. Save it as `repro.js`.
2. `npm i playwright@1.64.0 && npx playwright install chromium`
3. `node repro.js`

### Expected behavior

`Date.now() moved by 0 ms during one real second`: the clock is paused, only `runFor()`, `fastForward()`, `pauseAt()` or `resume()` should move it.

### Actual behavior

`Date.now() moved by 1003 ms during one real second`: on the second page the clock runs in real time.

Without the init script, or without the `runFor()` between the two navigations, it stays paused (0 ms).

### Additional context

What seems to happen, reading `packages/injected/src/clock.ts`:

- each clock call (`install`, `pauseAt`, `runFor`…) is recorded as a context init script that pushes an entry to the controller's log, and a new document replays that log lazily, at its first clock call (`_replayLogOnce`);
- on an existing page, init scripts run in the order they were added, so the second page runs the clock source, the `install` and `pauseAt` entries, the user init script, and only then the `runFor` entry;
- the user script's `Date.now()` replays the partial log `[install, pauseAt]`: paused. The `runFor` entry is pushed afterwards; at the next clock call it is replayed on its own, with no pause entry before it, so `_replayLogOnce` ends with `isPaused === false` and calls `_innerResume()`: real time.

A related symptom, same mechanism: `inject()` calls `controller.resume()` before the log entries are pushed, so until a document's first clock call its clock is in real-time mode. With `install()` the replay resets `ticks` to 0 and nothing shows. With `pauseAt()` called without `install()` (it installs the clock itself), it does: a page whose first script arrives 1.5 s after the document is created reads `performance.now()` ≈ 1416 at its first call, and `requestAnimationFrame` timestamps follow, while `Date.now()` is the paused time (1.63 and 1.64).

### Environment

```
- OS: macOS 27.0 (arm64)
- Node.js: 22.17.0
- Playwright: 1.64.0 (also 1.63.0)
- Browser: Chromium headless shell
```

