query:
[Bug]: `fill()` on `<input type=range>` throws "Malformed value" for valid numbers the browser accepts but re-serializes (e.g. `"0.850"`, `".5"`, `"1e-3"`)

### Version

1.64.0 (also reproduced on 1.62.1)

### Steps to reproduce

Repository: https://github.com/lumatic2/playwright-range-fill-repro

```sh
git clone https://github.com/lumatic2/playwright-range-fill-repro
cd playwright-range-fill-repro
npm install   # installs playwright@1.64.0 + Chromium
npm test      # runs repro.js below
```

`repro.js` (standalone, no test runner needed):

```js
// repro.js — node repro.js
const { chromium } = require('playwright');
(async () => {
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.setContent('<input type=range min=0 max=1 step=.001 value=0>');
  for (const v of ['0.850', '0.85', '1e-3', '+0.5', '.5', '0.5']) {
    try {
      await page.fill('input', v);
      console.log(JSON.stringify(v), 'OK   ->', await page.$eval('input', i => i.value));
    } catch (e) {
      console.log(JSON.stringify(v), 'FAIL ->', e.message.split('\n')[0]);
    }
  }
  // The browser itself accepts the same string and just normalizes it:
  console.log('DOM: value="0.850" ->', await page.evaluate(() => {
    const i = document.querySelector('input'); i.value = '0.850'; return i.value;
  }));
  await browser.close();
})();
```

Output:

```
"0.850" FAIL -> page.fill: Error: Malformed value
"0.85"  OK   -> 0.85
"1e-3"  FAIL -> page.fill: Error: Malformed value
"+0.5"  FAIL -> page.fill: Error: Malformed value
".5"    FAIL -> page.fill: Error: Malformed value
"0.5"   OK   -> 0.5
DOM: value="0.850" -> 0.85
```

### Expected behavior

`fill()` should accept any string that the browser accepts as a valid value for the range input. All five strings above are valid floating-point numbers per the HTML spec, are within `[min, max]`, and are on the `step` grid; setting them via `input.value = ...` succeeds and the browser merely re-serializes them to the canonical form (`0.850` → `0.85`). I'd expect `fill('0.850')` to behave exactly like `fill('0.85')`.

"Malformed value" should only be raised for values the browser actually rejects or clamps (`foo`, out-of-range `200`, off-step `15.43` — the existing cases in `tests/page/page-fill.spec.ts`).

### Actual behavior

`fill()` throws `Malformed value` for any string whose canonical serialization differs from the input string, even though the browser accepted it.

Cause: the injected `fill` sets `input.value = value` and then compares the result as a string (`packages/injected/src/injectedScript.ts`, "Malformed value" check):

https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/a3401cfe0dcf317101572d025fa46248d0779b42/packages/injected/src/injectedScript.ts#L1834

For `type=range`, the value sanitization algorithm is "parse as float, clamp to min/max, snap to step, serialize as a *valid floating-point number*", so the round-trip string is only identical when the caller already passes the canonical form. Trailing zeros, a leading `+`, a missing leading zero, or exponent notation all pass sanitization yet fail the string comparison.

This bites in practice whenever the value is produced by formatting code: `fill(t.toFixed(3))` (our case — a `step=.001` seek slider) fails for `0.850`, `0.100`, `1.000`, ... i.e. for 10% of all values, and the error message points at the value being malformed rather than at the comparison.

Workaround: normalize before filling, `fill(String(Number(t.toFixed(3))))`.

Suggested fix: for `range` compare numerically (`input.valueAsNumber !== Number(value)` / `Number(input.value) !== Number(value)`), or in general compare against the browser's own sanitization of the string rather than the raw string. Happy to send a PR with a regression test once the direction is agreed.

### Additional context

Only verified on Chromium; the comparison lives in the injected script, so it should be browser-independent.

### Environment

```
  System:
    OS: Windows 11 10.0.26200
    CPU: (32) x64 AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D 16-Core Processor
    Memory: 97.66 GB / 125.64 GB
  Binaries:
    Node: 24.14.1 - C:\Program Files\nodejs\node.EXE
    npm: 11.11.0 - C:\Program Files\nodejs\npm.CMD
  npmPackages:
    playwright: 1.64.0 (Chromium 156.0.8078.4)
```

