```

[Bug]: Directory setInputFiles can miss its input event and time out after native selection completes

### Version

Playwright 1.63.0; bundled WebKit 26.6; Node 24.20.0; Linux x86_64, Ubuntu Noble test image.

### Expected behavior

A directory `locator.setInputFiles()` call should finish once the genuine browser assignment and its input event finish, within the existing timeout.

### Actual behavior and observed boundary

An intermittent 30000ms `setInputFiles()` timeout occurred with two tiny synthetic files already selected and independently readable with exact expected bytes. Passive observation found one trusted `input` event and one `change` event. The input remained connected/enabled and the document complete.

A private native protocol capture established that the directory `DOM.setInputFiles` request was sent before the SDK input-listener evaluation request. The native setter acknowledged success; the exact SDK input-waiter evaluation remained unresolved until the timeout. This is not a claim that the listener request was sent after the setter response: the observed ordering is setter request before listener request, plus successful assignment and an unresolved waiter.

This reproduced on the 29th fresh WebKit page in a synthetic integration matrix; Chromium and Firefox each completed 40 fresh-page selections before it. Filesystem fixtures remained readable and were not removed until browser teardown. Raw traces and local paths are deliberately not attached.

### Source-level cause and proposed direction

The [1.63.0 directory branch](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/v1.63.0/packages/playwright-core/src/server/dom.ts) creates `waitForInputEvent` through asynchronous `this.evaluate(...).catch(...)` but does not await a listener-registration acknowledgement before `delegate.setInputFilePaths(...)`. JavaScript [evaluation setup](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/v1.63.0/packages/playwright-core/src/server/javascript.ts) can yield before the listener RPC is dispatched. The same unacknowledged ordering was present in main when inspected.

A local, test-only correction waits for `evaluateHandle` to return an object containing the event Promise: that acknowledges listener installation without prematurely awaiting the event. It then performs native assignment and awaits the retained event Promise under the original progress deadline. The existing context-loss handling is preserved and the wrapper handle is disposed without extending cleanup waits. No browser binary, public API, upload timeout or assertion was changed; no failed call was retried.

Passing-after evidence includes 40 fresh pages per native Chromium/Firefox/WebKit engine with exact directory/ZIP bytes, trusted events, missing-path refusal without selection mutation and owned teardown. The complete unchanged integration verification also passed. This is evidence for the correction, not a deterministic minimal reproduction or a claim that every environment exposes the race.

### Minimal SDK-only exercise and reproduction limit

The standalone exercise below uses only authored text files and genuine browser APIs. Install `playwright@1.63.0`, install its WebKit dependencies/browser, save as `directory-race.cjs`, then run with Node. It stops on the first failed upload and reads the independently selected bytes/events before closing the page; iterations are distinct fresh pages, not retries of a failed call.

**Important:** this reduced 400-page exercise completed without failure in one ordinary run and one private protocol-observed run. It is a minimal exercise for further reduction, not a claimed deterministic reproducer. The actual failing-before capture above came from the larger synthetic integration journey. I can work with maintainers to retain the acknowledged-registration invariant and design a hermetic regression without mocking native file selection or relying on probabilistic repetition.

```js
