query:
[Bug]: APIRequestContext multipart does not escape quotes/newlines in field and file names, producing a body servers cannot parse

### Version

1.64.1 (also reproduces on 1.65.0-alpha-2026-10-15)

### Steps to reproduce

Save as `repro.js` and run `node repro.js`. It only depends on `playwright`; the server side uses Node's built-in multipart parser (undici's `Request.formData()`):

```js
const http = require('http');
const { request } = require('playwright');

(async () => {
  const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
    const chunks = [];
    for await (const chunk of req)
      chunks.push(chunk);
    const body = Buffer.concat(chunks);
    console.log(body.toString().split('\r\n').filter(line => line.startsWith('content-disposition')).join('\n'));
    // Parse with Node's built-in multipart parser (undici).
    try {
      const form = await new Request('http://localhost/', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'content-type': req.headers['content-type'] }, body }).formData();
      console.log('parsed:', [...form.keys()]);
    } catch (e) {
      console.log('parse failed:', e.message);
    }
    res.end();
  });
  await new Promise(resolve => server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', resolve));

  const context = await request.newContext();
  await context.post(`http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/upload`, {
    multipart: {
      file: { name: 'report "final".txt', mimeType: 'text/plain', buffer: Buffer.from('hello') },
    },
  });
  await context.dispose();
  server.close();
})();
```

### Expected behavior

The quotes in the file name are escaped, as browsers do, and the server can parse the request:

```
content-disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="report %22final%22.txt"
parsed: [ 'file' ]
```

### Actual behavior

```
content-disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="report "final".txt"
parse failed: Failed to parse body as FormData.
```

Playwright writes the field name and the file name into the `Content-Disposition` header as-is. A `"`, CR or LF in either one ends the quoted string early, so the header is malformed. Node's parser then rejects **the whole request body**, not just that field. The same happens for:

- a field name containing a quote: `multipart: { 'say"hi': 'v1', other: 'v2' }`
- a file name containing a newline: `{ name: 'a\nb.txt', ... }`

For comparison, Chromium's `fetch()` with a `FormData` holding the same file and field encodes them as `filename="report %22final%22.txt"` and `name="say%22hi"`. The same parser accepts that and gives back the original names (`report "final".txt`, `say"hi`).

### Additional context

The HTML spec's [multipart/form-data encoding algorithm](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#multipart-form-data) requires escaping `"` as `%22`, CR as `%0D` and LF as `%0A` in field names and file names. The encoder in `packages/playwright-core/src/server/formData.ts` does no escaping:

```ts
this._chunks.push(Buffer.from(`; filename="${value.name}"`));
...
this._chunks.push(Buffer.from(`content-disposition: form-data; name="${name}"`));
```

I applied that escaping to both lines in a local 1.64.1 install. All three cases above then parse, and the server receives the original names.

This makes it impossible to use `APIRequestContext` to test upload endpoints with file names that contain quotes, which is a common case when testing file name sanitization. The encoding happens in the driver, so the other language bindings go through the same code.

### Environment

```
System:
  OS: Windows 11 10.0.26200
  CPU: (12) x64 12th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-1235U
  Memory: 15.68 GB
Binaries:
  Node: 22.14.0
  npm: 10.9.2
npmPackages:
  @playwright/test: 1.64.1
```

