[Bug]: clock.runFor() spends ~4.5 ms of real time per fired timer — 10,000 due timers take 45 s

### Version

1.63.0

### Steps to reproduce

```
npm i playwright@1.63.0
npx playwright install chromium
node repro.js
```

`repro.js`:

```js
const { chromium } = require('playwright');

const html = (kind, n) => `<script>
  window.done = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < ${n}; i++)
    ${kind === 'idle' ? 'requestIdleCallback(() => window.done++);' : 'setTimeout(() => window.done++, 0);'}
</script>`;

async function withoutClock(browser, kind, n) {
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  const start = Date.now();
  await page.setContent(html(kind, n));
  await page.waitForFunction(count => window.done === count, n, { polling: 10 });
  const ms = Date.now() - start;
  await page.close();
  return ms;
}

async function withClock(browser, kind, n) {
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-01-01T00:00:00Z') });
  await page.clock.pauseAt(new Date('2026-01-01T00:00:01Z'));
  await page.setContent(html(kind, n));
  const start = Date.now();
  await page.clock.runFor(1000);
  const ms = Date.now() - start;
  const done = await page.evaluate(() => window.done);
  await page.close();
  return { ms, done };
}

(async () => {
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  for (const kind of ['idle', 'timeout']) {
    for (const n of [1000, 10000]) {
      const real = await withoutClock(browser, kind, n);
      const fake = await withClock(browser, kind, n);
      console.log(`${kind === 'idle' ? 'requestIdleCallback' : 'setTimeout(fn, 0)'} x${n}: ` +
          `without clock ${real} ms | clock.runFor(1000) ${fake.ms} ms, ${fake.done} fired, ` +
          `${(fake.ms / fake.done).toFixed(2)} ms per timer`);
    }
  }
  await browser.close();
})();
```

### Expected behavior

`clock.runFor(1000)` fires the due callbacks in about the time the page needs to run them: without the clock, the same page runs 10,000 of them in ~25 ms.

### Actual behavior

Every fired timer costs ~4.5 ms of real time, whatever its type, so the cost grows linearly with the number of due timers:

```
requestIdleCallback x1000: without clock 23 ms | clock.runFor(1000) 4503 ms, 1000 fired, 4.50 ms per timer
requestIdleCallback x10000: without clock 26 ms | clock.runFor(1000) 45718 ms, 10000 fired, 4.57 ms per timer
setTimeout(fn, 0) x1000: without clock 12 ms | clock.runFor(1000) 4507 ms, 1000 fired, 4.51 ms per timer
setTimeout(fn, 0) x10000: without clock 21 ms | clock.runFor(1000) 45667 ms, 10000 fired, 4.57 ms per timer
```

On a real page that schedules one `requestIdleCallback` per link of a long list (about 10,000 links), a single `runFor(250)` takes more than a minute while the page's main thread is idle almost all of that time.

### Additional context

After every timer it fires, `_callFirstTimer` awaits a real `setTimeout` of the embedder:

- https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/754c170d8306ca89cb4cff9bf6a3184db09526a7/packages/injected/src/clock.ts#L377
- https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/754c170d8306ca89cb4cff9bf6a3184db09526a7/packages/injected/src/clock.ts#L393

Each yield is scheduled from inside the previous one, so after five levels the HTML timer nesting rule clamps it to 4 ms — which matches the ~4.5 ms per timer measured above.

Would a zero-delay yield between timers (a `MessageChannel` task, for example) be acceptable there, or does the clock need that 4 ms boundary between two timers?

### Environment

```shell
System: macOS 27.0.1, arm64 (Apple M3 Pro)
Node: 24.21.0
playwright: 1.63.0
Browser: Chromium 153.0.8010.12 (chromium-headless-shell, the default of chromium.launch())
```

