query:
[Bug]: A route added just after a `{ times: 1 }` route expires can resolve without intercepting

### Version

1.64.1

### Steps to reproduce

1. Clone https://github.com/T-Davis/playwright-route-race
2. `npm ci`
3. `npx playwright install chromium firefox webkit`
4. `npm test`, which runs `playwright test --repeat-each=100`

The whole test is below. It needs only `@playwright/test` and Node's built-in modules. The server answers `network` to any request that no route intercepts.

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import http from 'node:http';
import type { AddressInfo } from 'node:net';

// Answers every request that no route intercepts.
let server: http.Server;
let origin: string;

test.beforeAll(async () => {
  server = http.createServer((req, res) => res.end('network'));
  await new Promise<void>(resolve => server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', resolve));
  origin = `http://127.0.0.1:${(server.address() as AddressInfo).port}`;
});

test.afterAll(async () => {
  await new Promise(resolve => server.close(resolve));
});

const fetchApi = () => fetch('/api').then(response => response.text());

for (const gap of [0, 200]) {
  test(`route added ${gap} ms after a { times: 1 } route expires`, async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto(origin);

    await page.route('**/api', route => route.fulfill({ body: 'first' }), { times: 1 });
    expect(await page.evaluate(fetchApi)).toBe('first');

    if (gap)
      await page.waitForTimeout(gap);

    await page.route('**/api', route => route.fulfill({ body: 'second' }));
    expect(await page.evaluate(fetchApi), 'the second route never intercepted the request').toBe('second');
  });
}
```

### Expected behavior

Once `page.route()` resolves, its handler intercepts the next matching request. Both tests pass on every repeat.

### Actual behavior

The `0 ms` test fails intermittently in all three browsers. The second `page.route()` resolves, its handler never runs, and the request reaches the server.

```
Error: the second route never intercepted the request

Expected: "second"
Received: "network"
```

Failures in one `npm test` run, 100 repeats per browser, with the bundled browsers. Counts vary between runs. It also fails on `@next`, `1.65.0-alpha-2026-10-18` with its bundled browsers, at 0 ms only: WebKit 81, Chromium 7 and Firefox 6 of 100. I ran it only on macOS.

| Browser | `0 ms` | `200 ms` |
|---|---|---|
| WebKit | 81 | 0 |
| Chromium | 15 | 0 |
| Firefox | 12 | 0 |

### Additional context

This is my reading of the cause, at `b335271c`. One experiment below backs it.

When the `{ times: 1 }` handler finishes and no routes are left, `Page._onRoute` starts an update with an empty pattern list and does not await it. On `main` the call is `_disableInterception()`. In 1.64.1 it is `_updateInterceptionPatterns({ internal: true })`.
https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/b335271cdc2b57cac2be8ad37c5dd283ac6b8938/packages/playwright-core/src/client/page.ts#L226-L228

The page dispatcher's `setNetworkInterceptionPatterns` is not serialized, and `DispatcherConnection.dispatch` does not queue calls to the same object.
https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/b335271cdc2b57cac2be8ad37c5dd283ac6b8938/packages/playwright-core/src/server/dispatchers/pageDispatcher.ts#L209-L222
https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/b335271cdc2b57cac2be8ad37c5dd283ac6b8938/packages/playwright-core/src/server/page.ts#L709-L716

1. The empty update sees `hadMatchers` true and calls `removeRequestInterceptor`. That takes the interceptor out of the list before its first await. `_interceptionUrlMatchers` is not cleared until the await returns.
2. The next `page.route()` sends its update during that await. It also sees `hadMatchers` true, so it stores its pattern and skips `addRequestInterceptor`. The pattern is now stored with no interceptor installed.
3. The empty update resumes and sets `_interceptionUrlMatchers` to `[]`.

The experiment: I wrapped the page dispatcher's `setNetworkInterceptionPatterns` in `lib/coreBundle.js` in a per-dispatcher promise chain, so each call waits for the one before it. The `0 ms` test then failed 0 of 100 times in each browser.

`context.route()` races the same way. The same test with `page.context().route()` failed at `0 ms` 84 times in WebKit, 8 in Chromium and 13 in Firefox, out of 100 each. It never failed at `200 ms`.
https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/b335271cdc2b57cac2be8ad37c5dd283ac6b8938/packages/playwright-core/src/client/browserContext.ts#L257-L259
https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/b335271cdc2b57cac2be8ad37c5dd283ac6b8938/packages/playwright-core/src/server/dispatchers/browserContextDispatcher.ts#L315-L328

This looks different from #41802, though both start when a `{ times: 1 }` route expires and interception is turned off. That one was a CDP race in Chromium where the next request hung, and Chromium fixed it. Here the request is not intercepted at all and reaches the network, in all three browsers.

A workaround is to keep one route for the page's life and decide inside the handler, rather than adding a `{ times: 1 }` route per request.

### Environment

The Node and npm paths and the IDEs section are removed.

```shell
  System:
    OS: macOS 26.5.1
    CPU: (10) arm64 Apple M2 Pro
    Memory: 88.48 MB / 32.00 GB
  Binaries:
    Node: 24.21.0
    npm: 11.19.0
  Languages:
    Bash: 3.2.57 - /bin/bash
  npmPackages:
    @playwright/test: 1.64.1 => 1.64.1
```

